data.content:
Here's your test rewritten with specific MSTest 3.x collection assertions:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.IsNotEmpty(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    Assert.HasCount(3, users);
    var admin = Assert.ContainsSingle(users.Where(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**What changed:**

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsNotEmpty(users)` | Clearer intent, better failure message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.HasCount(3, users)` | Reports expected vs actual count on failure |
| `.Single()` + `Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)` | `Assert.ContainsSingle(...)` | Asserts exactly one match and returns it |

The `Assert.IsTrue(users.All(...))` line is fine to keep — there's no built-in "all match predicate" assertion in MSTest, so `IsTrue` is appropriate there. If you need the failure to identify *which* user wasn't active, you could loop with individual asserts instead.
